    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "With 3 lands available (Breeding Pool for G/U, Hushwood Verge for G/W, Hallowed Fountain for W/U), the player could cast Doc Aurlock (GU) or Badgermole Cub (1G) or Airbender Ascension (1W) instead of Llanowar Elves. The opponent has 3 lands with only 2 untapped (Island and Swamp), meaning they likely have Essence Scatter/counter mana available. Playing Llanowar Elves into potential counter mana is less punishing than losing a 2-drop like Doc Aurlock, but the opponent already used Essence Scatter on a previous Doc Aurlock (it's in both graveyards). Playing Llanowar Elves first is a reasonable 'test the waters' play, but the player could have led with Airbender Ascension (a noncreature spell that dodges Essence Scatter) to get value and then followed up with Llanowar Elves. With 1W available after tapping for Airbender Ascension (tap Hushwood Verge for W, Hallowed Fountain for W... no, need 1W = 2 mana). Actually: tap Breeding Pool for G, Hallowed Fountain for W = cast Airbender Ascension for 1W? No, 1W needs one generic + one white. Tap Breeding Pool (G) + Hallowed Fountain (W) = GW, which pays 1W. Then Hushwood Verge is untapped but only produces G (no Forest/Plains in type... wait, Breeding Pool is Forest Island, so Hushwood Verge can tap for W or G). So after casting Airbender Ascension, Hushwood Verge could tap for G to cast Llanowar Elves. This line gets both Airbender Ascension (dodges creature counters) AND Llanowar Elves on the same turn, which is strictly better than just Llanowar Elves alone.",
      "action_taken": "Cast only Llanowar Elves for G, leaving 2 mana unused",
